{{!-- Ian, 2026-09-23: "Make a more full explanation of what Ask is ...
     Explain what it does and doesn't do." In the thin-line style: a
     statement, then two cards, then the door. House voice: no comma
     splices, nothing tacked on with a comma, no em dashes. --}}
<div class="faith-ask-door">
  <h2 class="faith-ask-door-title">Ask the library a question.</h2>
  <p class="faith-ask-door-body">Ask answers from the works in The Faith Received and from nothing else. Every answer names the passages it drew on and links to each one in the reader. It is a way into the books through the questions you bring to them.</p>
  <div class="faith-ask-door-cards">
    <section class="faith-ask-door-card">
      <h3>What it does</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>It searches only the works in this library.</li>
        <li>It cites specific passages in real works. Each citation opens the reader at the passage it quotes.</li>
        <li>It shows you where to begin reading on a question.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="faith-ask-door-card">
      <h3>What it does not do</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>It does not search the internet.</li>
        <li>It is not the final word on any question. That weight belongs to the works it cites.</li>
        <li>It should not be cited itself. Cite the passage it led you to.</li>
        <li>It can be wrong. Read the passage before you rely on the answer.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
  <a class="fr-plan-open" href="/the-faith-received/ask/" data-feature-gate="ask">Open Ask</a>
</div>
